  {"title": "The rule behind every closed shape",
   "teach": "A full turn is 360 degrees. Walking a closed shape means you finish facing the way you started, so all your turns have to add to exactly 360. Divide 360 by the number of corners and you have the turn for each one.</p><p>That is the function from the opener, so here it is typed in and used. The loop calls <code>turn_size</code> once per pass, keeps what it hands back under the name <code>turn</code>, and prints it. The call has to land somewhere: on its own line, with nothing to catch the value, it would run and throw the answer away.",
   "code": 'def turn_size(sides):\n    return 360 / sides\n\nfor sides in range(3, 9):\n    turn = turn_size(sides)\n    print(f"{sides} sides -> {turn} degrees per turn")',
   "run": "py",
   "predict": "A triangle has three corners. Is the turn at each corner 60 degrees or 120?",
   "reveal": "120. The inside angle of an equilateral triangle is 60, but the turn you make walking around the outside is 120. This trips up nearly everyone once, and it is the reason we compute the turn rather than remember it."},

  {"title": "Where a step lands",
   "teach": 'Every spot in a window has two numbers, an x and a y. That is the same idea as a point on a coordinate grid in maths, with one difference: y counts down from the top, not up from the bottom. The corner (0, 0) is top left.</p><p>Which way you are facing is called your <b>heading</b>. It is an angle in degrees. 0 means facing right. Turning clockwise makes the number grow, so 90 faces straight down and 180 faces left.</p><p>A <b>step</b> means walking a set number of pixels in the direction you face. <code>pygame.draw.line</code> needs both ends of the line. You know the start and the heading, so the far end has to be worked out.</p><p>A diagonal step is part across and part down. <code>math.cos</code> tells you how much of the step is across, and <code>math.sin</code> tells you how much is down. You do not need to know how they work inside. You only need to know that they split a step into those two parts.</p><p>cos and sin measure angles in a unit called radians, not degrees. So <code>math.radians</code> converts first, the way you would convert inches to centimetres before using a metric ruler. The function then hands back both numbers at once, separated by a comma.</p><p><code>step_end</code> takes four values, in this order: <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, where the step starts; <code>heading</code>, which way it goes; and <code>length</code>, how far. In the four calls below, the start is always (100, 100) and the length is always 50. Only the heading changes, so any difference in the answers comes from the heading alone.</p><pre class="anatomy">step_end(100, 100, 0, 50)\n         <span class="lbl lbl-c">x    y</span>  <span class="lbl lbl-b">heading</span> <span class="lbl">length</span>\n\n<span class="lbl lbl-c">x, y      where the step starts</span>\n<span class="lbl lbl-b">heading   which way it goes</span>\n<span class="lbl">length    how far it goes</span></pre><p>',
   "figure": '<svg viewBox="0 0 520 250" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="A window: x grows across, y grows down, a step at heading 45 split into an across part and a down part" style="max-width:520px;display:block;margin:0 0 16px;font-family:JetBrains Mono,monospace;font-size:13px"><rect x="20" y="20" width="300" height="210" fill="#FFFDF7" stroke="#D3CBB8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="28" y="40" fill="#4A4D53">(0, 0)</text><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="120" y2="20" stroke="#0E4D52" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="120,15 130,20 120,25" fill="#0E4D52"/><text x="136" y="16" fill="#0E4D52" font-weight="700">x grows across</text><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="20" y2="120" stroke="#0E4D52" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="15,120 20,130 25,120" fill="#0E4D52"/><text x="30" y="130" fill="#0E4D52" font-weight="700">y grows</text><text x="30" y="146" fill="#0E4D52" font-weight="700">down</text><circle cx="150" cy="90" r="5" fill="#17181B"/><text x="112" y="80" fill="#17181B">start (x, y)</text><line x1="150" y1="90" x2="240" y2="90" stroke="#9C2B22" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><line x1="150" y1="90" x2="235" y2="175" stroke="#D99B12" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="235" cy="175" r="5" fill="#17181B"/><text x="244" y="180" fill="#17181B">far end</text><path d="M 180 90 A 30 30 0 0 1 171 111" fill="none" stroke="#9C2B22" stroke-width="2"/><text x="184" y="114" fill="#9C2B22" font-weight="700">45</text><line x1="150" y1="196" x2="235" y2="196" stroke="#1D5B39" stroke-width="3"/><text x="120" y="216" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">across: length * cos</text><line x1="262" y1="90" x2="262" y2="175" stroke="#1D5B39" stroke-width="3"/><text x="270" y="128" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">down:</text><text x="270" y="144" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">length</text><text x="270" y="160" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">* sin</text><text x="340" y="60" fill="#4A4D53">heading 0: faces right</text><text x="340" y="80" fill="#4A4D53">heading 90: faces down</text><text x="340" y="100" fill="#4A4D53">heading 180: faces left</text><text x="340" y="126" fill="#4A4D53">turning clockwise</text><text x="340" y="142" fill="#4A4D53">makes the number grow</text><text x="340" y="172" fill="#9C2B22" font-weight="700">this step: heading 45</text></svg>',
   "code": 'import math\n\ndef step_end(x, y, heading, length):\n    end_x = x + length * math.cos(math.radians(heading))\n    end_y = y + length * math.sin(math.radians(heading))\n    return round(end_x, 1), round(end_y, 1)\n\nprint(f"heading 0   {step_end(100, 100, 0, 50)}")\nprint(f"heading 90  {step_end(100, 100, 90, 50)}")\nprint(f"heading 180 {step_end(100, 100, 180, 50)}")\nprint(f"heading 45  {step_end(100, 100, 45, 50)}")',
   "run": "py",
   "predict": "A heading of 90 degrees. Does the step go up the screen or down it?",
   "figure_a": '<svg viewBox="0 0 640 370" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Four steps of length 50 from the point 100, 100: heading 0 lands at 150, 100 to the right; heading 90 lands at 100, 150 below; heading 180 lands at 50, 100 to the left; heading 45 lands at 135.4, 135.4 down and to the right" style="max-width:640px;display:block;margin:0 0 16px;font-family:JetBrains Mono,monospace;font-size:13px"><rect x="30" y="30" width="320" height="320" fill="#FFFDF7" stroke="#D3CBB8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="36" y="48" fill="#4A4D53">(0, 0)</text><text x="36" y="342" fill="#4A4D53">y grows down</text><text x="240" y="48" fill="#4A4D53">x grows across</text><line x1="190.0" y1="190.0" x2="270.0" y2="190.0" stroke="#0E4D52" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="270.0" cy="190.0" r="5" fill="#0E4D52"/><line x1="190.0" y1="190.0" x2="190.0" y2="270.0" stroke="#9C2B22" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="190.0" cy="270.0" r="5" fill="#9C2B22"/><line x1="190.0" y1="190.0" x2="110.0" y2="190.0" stroke="#8A5A00" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="110.0" cy="190.0" r="5" fill="#8A5A00"/><line x1="190.0" y1="190.0" x2="246.6" y2="246.6" stroke="#1D5B39" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="246.6" cy="246.6" r="5" fill="#1D5B39"/><text x="280" y="195" fill="#0E4D52" font-weight="700">(150, 100)</text><text x="150" y="292" fill="#9C2B22" font-weight="700">(100, 150)</text><text x="68" y="178" fill="#8A5A00" font-weight="700">(50, 100)</text><text x="257" y="252" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">(135.4, 135.4)</text><circle cx="190.0" cy="190.0" r="6" fill="#17181B"/><text x="150" y="166" fill="#17181B" font-weight="700">start (100, 100)</text><text x="380" y="60" fill="#4A4D53">Each step is 50 long.</text><text x="380" y="100" fill="#0E4D52" font-weight="700">heading 0: right</text><text x="380" y="130" fill="#9C2B22" font-weight="700">heading 90: down</text><text x="380" y="160" fill="#8A5A00" font-weight="700">heading 180: left</text><text x="380" y="190" fill="#1D5B39" font-weight="700">heading 45: down and right</text><text x="380" y="230" fill="#4A4D53">The four lines match the</text><text x="380" y="248" fill="#4A4D53">four printed pairs above.</text></svg>',
   "reveal": "Down. The second coordinate grows downward in a window, so a positive turn goes clockwise and a heading of 90 points at the floor. Leave out <code>math.radians</code> and nothing crashes: <code>math.cos(90)</code> is a real number, about negative 0.45, and your shape comes out as a scribble. That is a silent bug with no error message."},

  {"title": "A window you can draw on",
   "teach": "This is the first program of the term that opens a window. The lines before the drawing set it up. <code>set_mode</code> opens a window 640 wide and 480 tall. <code>canvas</code> is a blank picture of the same size, kept in memory, and <code>canvas.fill(BG)</code> paints it one colour. Every colour here is three numbers: how much red, green, and blue, each from 0 to 255.</p><p>The drawing happens once, onto the canvas, above the loop. <code>pygame.draw.line</code> draws on the canvas, not on the window.</p><p>The loop at the bottom has one job: copy the canvas onto the window sixty times a second until you close the window. Copy the loop as it stands. Session 10 takes it apart line by line.",
   "code": 'import math\nimport pygame\n\nWIDTH = 640\nHEIGHT = 480\nBG = (27, 41, 81)\nYELLOW = (252, 211, 77)\n\ndef step_end(x, y, heading, length):\n    end_x = x + length * math.cos(math.radians(heading))\n    end_y = y + length * math.sin(math.radians(heading))\n    return end_x, end_y\n\npygame.init()\nscreen = pygame.display.set_mode((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\ncanvas = pygame.Surface((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\ncanvas.fill(BG)\n\n# ---- your drawing, done once ----\nend_x, end_y = step_end(100, 100, 30, 200)\nprint(f"line runs from (100, 100) to ({round(end_x, 1)}, {round(end_y, 1)})")\npygame.draw.line(canvas, YELLOW, (100, 100), (end_x, end_y), 3)\n\n# ---- the window loop: hold the picture on screen until the window closes ----\nrunning = 1\nwhile running:\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = 0\n    screen.blit(canvas, (0, 0))\n    pygame.display.flip()\n    clock.tick(60)\n\npygame.quit()',
   "run": "headless",
   "frames": 5,
   "predict": 'The line starts at (100, 100), faces heading 30, and is 200 long. Before you run: does the far end sit to the right or the left of the start, and above or below it? Then check the two printed numbers against your answer.',
   "hint": ['Heading 0 faces right and heading 90 faces down, so 30 is somewhere between those two.'],
   "reveal": "Right and below. Heading 30 is a third of the way from facing right to facing down, so the step is mostly across and partly down. The printed numbers agree: x went from 100 to 273.2, which is 173.2 across, and y went from 100 to 200.0, which is 100 down. Half of 200 is 100, so at heading 30 a step goes down by exactly half its length. The last value given to <code>draw.line</code>, the 3, is the thickness in pixels; leave it out and you get a line one pixel wide, with no error."},

  {"title": "A function that draws any polygon",
   "teach": "<code>polygon</code> takes seven values, the most of any function in this class session. In order: the canvas to draw on, the colour, x and y for where to start, the heading to face first, how many sides, and how long each side is.</p><p>Think of a pen on the canvas. Each pass of the loop draws one side, moves the pen to the far end of that side, and turns by <code>turn</code> degrees. After the last side the function hands back where the pen is and which way it faces.",
   "figure": '<pre class="anatomy">polygon(canvas, YELLOW, 320, 240, 0, 6, 80)\n        <span class="lbl">canvas  colour  <span class="lbl-c">x    y</span>  <span class="lbl-b">heading sides length</span></span>\n\n<span class="lbl">canvas, colour   what to draw on, and in what colour</span>\n<span class="lbl lbl-c">x, y             where the pen starts</span>\n<span class="lbl lbl-b">heading          which way the pen faces first</span>\n<span class="lbl lbl-b">sides            how many sides the shape has</span>\n<span class="lbl lbl-b">length           how long each side is</span></pre>',

   "prefix": 'import math\nimport pygame\nWIDTH = 640\nHEIGHT = 480\nBG = (27, 41, 81)\nYELLOW = (252, 211, 77)\ndef step_end(x, y, heading, length):\n    end_x = x + length * math.cos(math.radians(heading))\n    end_y = y + length * math.sin(math.radians(heading))\n    return end_x, end_y\npygame.init()\nscreen = pygame.display.set_mode((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\ncanvas = pygame.Surface((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\ncanvas.fill(BG)\n',
   "code": 'def polygon(canvas, color, x, y, heading, sides, length):\n    turn = 360 / sides\n    for i in range(sides):\n        end_x, end_y = step_end(x, y, heading, length)\n        pygame.draw.line(canvas, color, (x, y), (end_x, end_y), 3)\n        x = end_x\n        y = end_y\n        heading = heading + turn\n    return x, y, heading\n\nx, y, heading = polygon(canvas, YELLOW, 320, 240, 0, 6, 80)\nprint(f"pen finished at ({round(x, 1)}, {round(y, 1)}) facing {round(heading, 1)}")',
   "run": "headless",
   "frames": 3,
   "predict": 'The hexagon started at 320, 240 facing 0. Where does the pen finish, and which way is it facing?',
   "hint": ['Think about walking around a shape on the ground. When you have gone all the way round, where are you standing?', 'A hexagon has 6 sides, so the turn at each corner is 360 / 6 = 60, and the loop adds that turn to the heading six times. Add the six turns up.'],
   "reveal": "Back at 320, 240, facing 360. The turns added to 360 and the sides were all the same length, so the pen walked a closed loop. Facing 360 is the same direction as facing 0, because one full turn brings you back to where you started. Session 8 shows a way to make Python say 0 instead of 360."},

